1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,937
Chapter 1.

2
00:00:00,937 --> 00:00:02,337
A Wound in the World.

3
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:06,475
The march across Serephis was an exercise in unraveling.

4
00:00:06,475 --> 00:00:17,412
Not of the body, though the heat fused sand into sheets of black glass beneath his sabatons and the sky hung a color no healthy sky had any business wearing.

5
00:00:17,412 --> 00:00:19,700
This was an unraveling of the soul.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:29,025
Miguel, General of the Celestial Host, knew the clean pain of battle — the bite of demonic steel, the burn of infernal fire.

7
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:33,312
Those were honest wounds, wounds with edges you could name and close.

8
00:00:33,312 --> 00:00:34,562
This was different.

9
00:00:34,562 --> 00:00:40,712
This was a hollow ache lodged in the center of his being, a space where something had been taken.

10
00:00:40,712 --> 00:00:47,850
His gauntleted hand went to his chest by instinct, a gesture he had repeated a thousand times since the ache began.

11
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:55,537
He was fundamentally incomplete, and one question drove him across the blistering wastes: what was taken from me?

12
00:00:55,537 --> 00:00:59,112
He did not think of himself as a man chasing a weapon.

13
00:00:59,112 --> 00:01:08,887
He told himself he was chasing an answer — that the ache was a wound from some battle scrubbed from his memory, and that once he understood it, he could return to his life whole.

14
00:01:08,887 --> 00:01:12,725
He did not let himself examine how thin that hope had worn.

15
00:01:12,725 --> 00:01:19,100
The dune broke apart under his boot, and beneath the crust of glass-sand, Miguel found stone.

16
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:20,087
He stopped.

17
00:01:20,087 --> 00:01:28,150
Not because the wound in his chest had changed its pull — it hadn't, it never did — but because the stone was cut, not shattered.

18
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:35,637
A paving block, its edge still square after however many centuries the desert had spent trying to erase it.

19
00:01:35,637 --> 00:01:48,325
He crouched and brushed the sand away with two fingers, uncovering a second block beside the first, and a third beyond that, a buried road running straight toward the horizon he was already walking.

20
00:01:48,325 --> 00:01:51,075
Serephis had not always been empty.

21
00:01:51,075 --> 00:01:57,250
He knew this the way he knew most things about the deep past — as doctrine, not memory.

22
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:02,212
A kingdom here, before the Fall settled its architecture over everything older.

23
00:02:02,212 --> 00:02:03,987
He had never learned its name.

24
00:02:03,987 --> 00:02:22,000
Someone, at some council or another, had mentioned once that the desert itself was not a ruin but a weapon — that a people who could no longer win a war had unmade their own land rather than hand it to what was coming, had burned the water table and salted the air and let the wind do the rest for a thousand years.

25
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,825
He hadn't believed it at the time.

26
00:02:23,825 --> 00:02:29,287
It had sounded like the kind of story armies tell each other to make retreat sound like strategy.

27
00:02:29,287 --> 00:02:36,162
He believed it now, crouched over a road built for feet that had stopped walking it before his own kind had names.

28
00:02:36,162 --> 00:02:46,825
For a moment — brief, unwelcome — the ache in his chest answered the stone with a low, sympathetic throb, as though something in him recognized something in it.

29
00:02:46,825 --> 00:02:51,987
A land that chose its own ending rather than survive as someone else's territory.

30
00:02:51,987 --> 00:03:00,775
He did not like the shape of that thought, so he stood, and did not look down again, and let the buried road vanish back beneath his next stride.

31
00:03:00,775 --> 00:03:05,750
For days the ache had been a dull throb, easy enough to bury beneath duty.

32
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:12,362
Now it pulsed with a clear, directional authority, dragging him forward whether he willed it or not.

33
00:03:12,362 --> 00:03:21,537
It reminded him, cruelly, of the clarity of the Divine Word — the way true command settled into the soul as unarguable fact.

34
00:03:21,537 --> 00:03:23,737
This new call wore the same certainty.

35
00:03:23,737 --> 00:03:25,662
Its origin terrified him.

36
00:03:25,662 --> 00:03:31,625
He stopped, grit crunching under his boots, and closed his eyes a moment against the fear of it.

37
00:03:31,625 --> 00:03:38,862
When he opened them, he had already turned toward the pull, a body surrendering before the mind agreed to it.

38
00:03:38,978 --> 00:03:42,928
The oppressive stillness broke on a chord of pure harmony.

39
00:03:42,928 --> 00:03:50,028
Gabriel had arrived, and his presence was a physical relief, a sudden coolness on sun-scorched skin.

40
00:03:50,028 --> 00:03:55,265
Soft light spread around his brother, dispelling the gloom for a pace.

41
00:03:55,265 --> 00:04:00,240
Miguel felt love, sharp enough to hurt, and close behind it, shame.

42
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:12,365
He did not want Gabriel — the Herald of Sacred Resonance, keeper of a thousand tongues — to see him like this: unraveled, hunting a phantom ache across a cursed waste.

43
00:04:12,365 --> 00:04:19,253
He straightened, a futile attempt at a commander's bearing, but the weariness was carved too deep to hide.

44
00:04:19,253 --> 00:04:21,790
Gabriel's face held no judgment.

45
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:25,965
To Miguel it looked like something worse: a raw, aching worry.

46
00:04:25,965 --> 00:04:28,203
"The Council is concerned, Miguel."

47
00:04:28,203 --> 00:04:33,965
Gabriel's voice carried that same first harmony, but strained now with an audible plea.

48
00:04:33,965 --> 00:04:39,215
"They fear this is a deception — a lure the enemy crafted to draw you out.

49
00:04:39,215 --> 00:04:41,640
Your absence puts fear in the Host."

50
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:47,428
The words — Council, duty, fear — arrived like echoes from another life.

51
00:04:47,428 --> 00:04:50,865
Beside the ache in his chest, they seemed irrelevant.

52
00:04:50,865 --> 00:04:53,765
The ache flared with fresh heat regardless.

53
00:04:53,765 --> 00:04:59,028
Miguel took a half-step back, an arm crossing instinctively over his ribs.

54
00:04:59,028 --> 00:05:03,990
"They think me a fool," Miguel said, his voice rough with disuse.

55
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:05,115
"Or a traitor."

56
00:05:05,115 --> 00:05:06,790
"They think you are their commander.

57
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:08,465
Lost, and in pain."

58
00:05:08,465 --> 00:05:10,315
Gabriel's voice gentled.

59
00:05:10,315 --> 00:05:12,053
"They think of their brother."

60
00:05:12,053 --> 00:05:15,465
Something else rode with his brother that Gabriel did not name.

61
00:05:15,465 --> 00:05:31,415
Miguel caught the edge of it as Gabriel shifted his weight — a folded parchment tucked close against his ribs, sealed in wax too dark and too formal for a simple message, the kind of seal that belonged to a Council order rather than a brother's private word.

62
00:05:31,415 --> 00:05:34,403
Gabriel made no move to produce it.

63
00:05:34,403 --> 00:05:42,215
He did not so much as glance toward it, and something in that careful stillness told Miguel more than the parchment itself could have.

64
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:48,703
Whatever it authorized, Gabriel had apparently decided, on his own, not to need it yet.

65
00:05:48,703 --> 00:05:54,715
The shift — envoy to brother — affected Miguel more deeply than any order could have.

66
00:05:54,715 --> 00:05:59,153
For one shared breath, he forgot everything beyond the two of them.

67
00:05:59,153 --> 00:06:05,903
There was a version of this moment Miguel had lived before, and it was the reason he could not make himself turn around.

68
00:06:05,903 --> 00:06:22,090
Onyx Gates had cost him his wing — a siege that had gone wrong in the specific, catastrophic way sieges go wrong when solid ground stops being solid, and he had come out of it on his back in a collapsing fortress, unable to feel the arm that should have been screaming.

69
00:06:22,090 --> 00:06:23,590
Gabriel had found him there.

70
00:06:23,590 --> 00:06:29,940
Not sent — found, on his own, having broken from a command post he had no business leaving.

71
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:53,515
Three nights he had knelt beside him in the dark, cauterizing what could be cauterized with nothing but his own light and his own hands, talking the entire time about nothing — old songs, a joke about Uriel's temper, the taste of something they'd once eaten on a world neither of them could name anymore — because silence, he'd said afterward, was the thing that let wounds decide they were fatal.

72
00:06:53,515 --> 00:06:59,865
Miguel had asked him, once the fortress finally stopped groaning, why he hadn't sent a healer instead.

73
00:06:59,865 --> 00:07:04,853
Because you don't yell for help in the voice you use for people you love, Gabriel had said.

74
00:07:04,853 --> 00:07:06,053
You go yourself.

75
00:07:06,053 --> 00:07:08,540
He heard that voice now, under the pleading.

76
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:15,540
The same low register, the same tenderness that, to Miguel, seemed to conceal exhaustion.

77
00:07:15,540 --> 00:07:21,965
It would have been easier if Gabriel had come as an envoy, all official grief and official warning.

78
00:07:21,965 --> 00:07:33,365
He had come instead as the brother who once spent three nights on a burning floor for him, and that was a debt Miguel could not simply outwalk, however strongly the wound pulled him onward.

79
00:07:33,365 --> 00:07:34,765
He kept walking anyway.

80
00:07:34,765 --> 00:07:37,728
That, too, felt like something he would owe for later.

81
00:07:37,728 --> 00:07:50,303
Miguel was thrown back to the ruin of that memory now, standing here in a different waste with a different wound, and his jaw, clenched for days, loosened for one unbearable second.

82
00:07:50,303 --> 00:08:00,753
Gabriel's gaze softened; his hand rose, hesitated, and fell back to his side, the gesture unfinished, the history between them heavier for it.

83
00:08:00,866 --> 00:08:02,378
"Come home," Gabriel said.

84
00:08:02,378 --> 00:08:05,753
"Whatever this wound is, we face it together.

85
00:08:05,753 --> 00:08:07,453
As we always have."

86
00:08:07,453 --> 00:08:09,328
For a moment Miguel wavered.

87
00:08:09,328 --> 00:08:16,128
The thought of the Bastion, of home's familiar harmonies, sang like a promise he had almost forgotten how to want.

88
00:08:16,128 --> 00:08:24,641
Then came the colder thought: returning would mean carrying this void for eternity, a permanent reminder of his incompletion.

89
00:08:24,641 --> 00:08:27,428
The Council would offer comfort, not answers.

90
00:08:27,428 --> 00:08:30,528
They would try to heal a wound they could not even see.

91
00:08:30,528 --> 00:08:31,803
He had to go forward.

92
00:08:31,803 --> 00:08:32,741
He had to know.

93
00:08:32,741 --> 00:08:33,566
"I cannot."

94
00:08:33,566 --> 00:08:43,241
He met his brother's eyes for the first time since Gabriel had arrived — his own gaze no longer weary, but lit with a terrible, crystalline resolve.

95
00:08:43,241 --> 00:08:47,966
"This call is a command more fundamental than any the Council can give me.

96
00:08:47,966 --> 00:08:50,903
It is a truth I must find, or I am nothing."

97
00:08:50,903 --> 00:08:54,216
He turned, angling his body toward the pull.

98
00:08:54,216 --> 00:08:56,278
The choice had already made itself.

99
00:08:56,278 --> 00:08:57,566
He was alone now.

100
00:08:57,566 --> 00:09:04,641
Gabriel's light dimmed a fraction, harmony faltering into something like a held note that would not resolve.

101
00:09:04,641 --> 00:09:06,716
He offered no further argument.

102
00:09:06,716 --> 00:09:09,953
His message was delivered; his plea, spent.

103
00:09:09,953 --> 00:09:18,653
A single tear of pure light traced his cheek, and he inclined his head, sorrow written plain across features that rarely showed it.

104
00:09:18,653 --> 00:09:20,703
"May you find what you seek, brother."

105
00:09:20,703 --> 00:09:22,791
His voice had thickened with grief.

106
00:09:22,791 --> 00:09:31,028
Then he was gone, folded back into the space between worlds, the sealed parchment still riding unmentioned against his ribs.

107
00:09:31,028 --> 00:09:38,153
Miguel found himself, watching the space where his brother had stood, wondering what exactly he had just been spared.

108
00:09:38,153 --> 00:09:43,891
The heat and hush of Serephis returned, intensified by Gabriel's absence.

109
00:09:43,891 --> 00:09:49,778
The memory of his brother's sorrowful eyes became one more weight for Miguel to carry.

110
00:09:49,778 --> 00:09:58,228
Miguel let the regret surface once, fully, before setting it aside; only the certainty of the choice itself remained.

111
00:09:58,228 --> 00:09:59,503
The choice was made.

112
00:09:59,503 --> 00:10:01,166
He turned and marched on.

113
00:10:01,166 --> 00:10:06,828
He walked an age through unchanging desert, until, without warning, it changed.

114
00:10:06,828 --> 00:10:14,841
The transition struck like a physical blow: one step on hot, grating sand, the next on cool, yielding moss.

115
00:10:14,841 --> 00:10:21,916
The oppressive air gave way to a still coolness that carried a metallic tang, like the moment after lightning.

116
00:10:21,916 --> 00:10:23,316
He had reached the grove.

117
00:10:23,316 --> 00:10:35,953
Trees with leaves of pale, leaf-thin metal rose in ranks around him, casting a bright, even light that fell on nothing — no shadows anywhere, a flat and depthless world.

118
00:10:35,953 --> 00:10:38,616
His tactical mind screamed ambush.

119
00:10:38,616 --> 00:10:46,128
Nothing in creation was made this perfectly; it felt manufactured, a lure dressed as a sanctuary.

120
00:10:46,128 --> 00:10:54,228
His hand drifted to the space where a sword hilt should have waited, an old habit that now only measured his own vulnerability.

121
00:10:54,228 --> 00:11:01,428
Beautiful as it was, this place held the answer he had come for, and he would not let its perfection deceive him.

122
00:11:01,428 --> 00:11:03,891
The quiet here felt watchful.

123
00:11:03,891 --> 00:11:08,516
It made him feel like an intruder in a place sacred but not holy.

124
00:11:08,516 --> 00:11:09,528
Wet stone.

125
00:11:09,528 --> 00:11:12,428
Something like the air after a lightning strike.

126
00:11:12,428 --> 00:11:17,003
No breeze stirred the leaves; the temperature never wavered.

127
00:11:17,003 --> 00:11:27,878
He tried to recite a verse of the Divine Song, the centering exercise he had used before a thousand battles, but the encompassing stillness left him unable to recall the words.

128
00:11:27,878 --> 00:11:33,966
Every soft crunch of fallen leaf beneath his boots felt like a shout inside a library.

129
00:11:33,966 --> 00:11:39,491
He could not shake the sense of being measured, though he had no idea by what, or for what.

130
00:11:39,602 --> 00:11:49,602
He felt it first as a change in the light rather than a shape — one of the metal trees, some forty paces off, no longer quite matching the angle of the others around it.

131
00:11:49,602 --> 00:12:03,552
He turned toward it fully and found a figure standing there, tall as the trees themselves, wrought of the same pale, leafless material, its face a smooth continuation of its shoulders, unbroken by feature of any kind.

132
00:12:03,552 --> 00:12:04,827
It did not move.

133
00:12:04,827 --> 00:12:10,339
It did not raise a weapon, though something in the set of its shoulders suggested it did not need one.

134
00:12:10,339 --> 00:12:18,827
Miguel had stood before ten thousand enemies in his long service and had learned to read intention in a stance before a single blow fell.

135
00:12:18,827 --> 00:12:21,277
He read nothing here he recognized.

136
00:12:21,277 --> 00:12:23,402
Not hostility, not welcome.

137
00:12:23,402 --> 00:12:30,939
Only attention, absolute and unblinking, and he found himself unable to guess what it was waiting to see.

138
00:12:30,939 --> 00:12:39,252
"I have come for what is mine," Miguel said, and heard, even as he said it, how little he actually knew of the claim he was making.

139
00:12:39,252 --> 00:12:41,839
The faceless figure offered no answer.

140
00:12:41,839 --> 00:12:50,289
Its stillness felt, somehow, like a reply anyway — one that left his own certainty feeling small and borrowed beside it.

141
00:12:50,289 --> 00:12:56,727
He took one step toward the grove's center, testing the silence for whatever line he was not meant to cross.

142
00:12:56,727 --> 00:12:57,889
No line came.

143
00:12:57,889 --> 00:13:00,489
The figure did not follow or block him.

144
00:13:00,489 --> 00:13:08,139
It merely turned, smooth and soundless, to keep facing him as he passed, like a compass needle holding north.

145
00:13:08,139 --> 00:13:12,889
It seemed content, as far as Miguel could tell, simply to watch him go.

146
00:13:12,889 --> 00:13:15,989
He did not have the luxury of stopping to guess what that meant.

147
00:13:15,989 --> 00:13:26,614
He walked on, the faceless figure's gaze a weight against his back long after the trees closed between them and hid it from sight, a weight he suspected he had not left behind at all.

148
00:13:26,614 --> 00:13:32,389
At the grove's center stood a great ash, its bark the color of cooled lava.

149
00:13:32,389 --> 00:13:38,239
Its roots reached into a pool of starlight so pure it looked poured rather than reflected.

150
00:13:38,239 --> 00:13:44,514
Sunk into the trunk was a sword — not forged metal, but living, solidified light.

151
00:13:44,514 --> 00:13:53,764
The instant he saw it, the ache in his chest went silent, the void filled by something that pulsed in the same rhythm as his own hollow wound.

152
00:13:53,764 --> 00:14:00,777
A name surfaced in his mind unbidden, not remembered so much as rediscovered: Solmire.

153
00:14:00,777 --> 00:14:07,064
It felt less like learning a word and more like finding one that had always belonged to him, waiting.

154
00:14:07,064 --> 00:14:12,727
His steps, cautious a moment before, now pulled him forward almost against his will.

155
00:14:12,727 --> 00:14:15,077
This was not the thought that is a sword.

156
00:14:15,077 --> 00:14:20,339
It was recognition, absolute and terrifying: that is what was taken from me.

157
00:14:20,339 --> 00:14:24,039
He stood before the ash, hand trembling as he raised it.

158
00:14:24,039 --> 00:14:29,139
The blade's presence pressed on him like an older, more fundamental truth.

159
00:14:29,139 --> 00:14:34,339
For a heartbeat, the name Miguel felt like a title someone else had given him to wear.

160
00:14:34,339 --> 00:14:42,264
A low hum built behind his eyes, not sound but vibration, resonant with the hollow space beneath his ribs.

161
00:14:42,264 --> 00:14:47,027
His fingers hovered inches from the hilt, caught between hunger and dread.

162
00:14:47,027 --> 00:14:50,652
If he touched this, would he still be himself afterward?

163
00:14:50,652 --> 00:14:52,914
Or only an extension of the blade's will?

164
00:14:52,914 --> 00:14:56,014
The moment skin met hilt, the grove vanished.

